            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 6,
              "start": 6
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice Kagan, the question presented is whether the pursuit of a fleeing misdemeanor suspect always qualifies as an exigent circumstance."
      }
    ],
    "heading": "Background and Question",
    "status": "complete",
    "summary": null,
    "summary_citations": []
  },
  "case_id": "2020-20-18",
  "decision": {
    "claims": [
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "fa24f9592f389f71ce6f887329d8930a2ee14ba12001b9947c660c96a9b654a8",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-fa24f9592f38-0001-0007"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 20,
              "start": 20
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The majority held that the common law does not support a categorical rule allowing warrantless home entry when a misdemeanant fees. It had a rule of that kind for felonies. But much as in Welsh centuries later, the common law made distinctions based on \u201cthe gravity of the underlying offense.\u201d"
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "fa24f9592f389f71ce6f887329d8930a2ee14ba12001b9947c660c96a9b654a8",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-fa24f9592f38-0001-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 11,
              "start": 11
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Court rejected the amicus\u2019s broad view that any suspect, felony or misdemeanor, could be entered under a categorical hot\u2011pursuit rule."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "fa24f9592f389f71ce6f887329d8930a2ee14ba12001b9947c660c96a9b654a8",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-fa24f9592f38-0001-0007"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 20,
              "start": 20
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Court noted that common law historically distinguished the scope of hot\u2011pursuit based on the gravity of the underlying offense."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "fa24f9592f389f71ce6f887329d8930a2ee14ba12001b9947c660c96a9b654a8",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-fa24f9592f38-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 21,
              "start": 21
            }
          },
          {
            "document_hash": "fa24f9592f389f71ce6f887329d8930a2ee14ba12001b9947c660c96a9b654a8",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-fa24f9592f38-0003-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 32,
              "start": 32
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Kavanaugh\u2019s concurrence clarified that an exigent circumstance includes a risk of escape, destruction of evidence, or harm to others, and that hot\u2011pursuit often gives rise to multiple exigencies."
      }
    ],
